A problem of Erd\H{o}s, Herzog, and Piranien \cite{EHP58}, who proved that if $F$ is the disc of radius $1$ then this set can have $n$ connected components (for example $f(z)=z^n+1$).

The transfinite diameter of $F$, also known as the logarithmic capacity, is defined by\[\rho(F)=\lim_{n\to \infty}\sup_{z_1,\ldots,z_n\in F}\left(\prod_{i<j}\lvert z_i-z_j\rvert\right)^{1/\binom{n}{2}}.\]This was solved by Ghosh and Ramachandran \cite{GhRa24}, who proved that, if $d$ is the transfinite diameter of $F$, then
{UL}
{LI}if $0<d<1$ then the set has at most $(1-c)n$ connected components for some $c>0$ depending on $F$;{/LI}
{LI}if $d\leq 1/4$ and $F$ is connected then the set has only one connected component;{/LI}
{LI}there are examples with $d=1$ such that, for infinitely many $n$, the set can have $n$ connected components.{/LI}
{/UL}
They also note that the answer cannot depend only on the transfinite diameter of $F$ - for example, both $F_1=\{ z: \lvert z\rvert\leq 1/2\}$ and $F_2=[-1,1]$ have transfinite diameter $1/2$, but the former always has one connected component, and the latter can have $\gg n$ many connected components.

